okay today I'm going to talk to you  about desalination so if a solution is  said to be saline it means it's salty  so what desalination means it's just  removing salt from water so we might  want to do this in order to create  drinking water so there's two main ways  that we need to know about in terms of  desalination one is thermal desalination  and the other one is by a process we're  going to learn about in a minute called  reverse osmosis so first of all thermal  desalination whenever we see the word  thermal in science we're thinking about  heat so the way that this works is a  processor might have come across in  chemistry called distillation so we  start off here with a mixture of salt  and water a saline solution remember the  whole thing that we're trying to achieve  is we're trying to remove the salt and  create drinking water clean water so  what we can do is we can actually heat  up this salty water in a lab you get it  with a Bunsen burner but in an industry  might do it in a big big boiler or  furnace but you actually by heating the  water you cause this water here to  evaporate so by evaporating it the salt  does not evaporate and it stays here but  the water does evaporate so it passes  down here so turned it to it from a  liquid to a gas and turns down here now  in the condenser what happens is the  steam actually condenses so remember  this term means turning from a gas back  into a liquid so we've so the water can  then drip down here so what we've done  is we've evaporated the water leaving  the salt behind then the steam because  this condensed is really nice and cool  it condenses and turns back from a gas  into a liquid and goes in here  this is now clean drinking water that we  can save the drink okay  the next process we're gonna look at is  reverse osmosis so hopefully we already  know a little bit about osmosis  so as Moses remember is the movement of  water particles from a high  concentration to a low concentration  across a partially permeable membrane so  I'm just going to draw the set up right  here  so the blue circles here what they  represent these are water molecules so  I've got two sides of my membrane one I  have a high concentration of water  molecules and the other one I have a  high / lower concentration of water  molecules because these represent these  green circles represents salt molecules  so again remember desalination means  removing salt from the water I want to  end up with just water and remove my  salt now in normal osmosis the higher  concentration of water is on this side  so in normal osmosis we have a net  movement from here to here from a high  concentration of water to a low  concentration of water across a  partially permeable membrane but in  reverse osmosis as the name suggests  it's the other way now the reason why  water moves the other way is a lot of  pressure is applied from this side right  here so there's pressure put on this  side right here now this causes instead  of in normal osmosis where we go from a  high concentration of water to a low  concentration of water we actually go  the other way so the water molecules  actually move this way now the reason  why the salt does not cross and go that  way is because the salt is too big to go  through our partially permeable membrane  that's what partially permeable means it  means it only lets certain things  through so in this case the water  molecules move from this side to this  side so the salt the stuff we don't want  is left behind and on this side all of  this water can be taken off  and removed for drinking water so just  to summarize desalination means removing  salt from water we could do this by  thermal desalination by evaporating the  water and condensing it again to  separate it from the salt or by reverse  osmosis where we apply pressure which  causes the water molecules to move  against the concentration gradient from  a low concentration of water to a high  concentration of water to create clean  drinking water thank you
 

